// Domain 4: Design Cost-Optimized Architectures (20%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d4-01", d: 4,
  q: "A company stores application log files in Amazon S3. Logs are read often during the first 30 days for troubleshooting, are read only a few times during the next 60 days, and after 90 days are kept only for audits, which can wait up to 2 days for a retrieval. Regulations require the logs to be kept for 7 years and then deleted.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that moves objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days and to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days. Expire the objects after 7 years.",
      "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that moves objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days and keeps them there. Expire the objects after 7 years to meet the retention rule.",
      "Store all objects in S3 Intelligent-Tiering from the start. Expire the objects after 7 years with an S3 Lifecycle rule.",
      "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that moves objects to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 1 day. Expire the objects after 7 years."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The rule matches each access phase to a cheaper class: Standard for active use, Standard-IA for occasional reads, and Deep Archive for audits that can wait up to 2 days. Expiration deletes the logs when the 7 years end.",
      "Keeping years of audit data in Standard-IA costs far more than an archive class for data that is almost never read.",
      "Intelligent-Tiering adds a monitoring charge for each object, and automatic tiers alone do not reach the lowest-cost archive class for the audit years.",
      "Moving logs after 1 day adds retrieval charges during the first 30 days when the logs are read often, and Instant Retrieval costs more than Deep Archive for the audit years."] },

{ id: "d4-02", d: 4,
  q: "A media company stores 2 PB of images in Amazon S3. Some images become popular again months after upload, and the company cannot predict which ones. Each image is larger than 1 MB. When an image is requested, it must load in milliseconds, and the company wants to lower storage costs without building any access tracking itself.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Move the images to S3 Intelligent-Tiering. Let it move each object between access tiers based on its own access pattern.",
      "Move the images to S3 Standard-IA. Accept the retrieval charge on each request to keep the storage price low.",
      "Move the images to S3 One Zone-IA. Copy any image that becomes popular back to S3 Standard with a scheduled job.",
      "Move the images to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval. Accept the retrieval charge on each request to keep the storage price low."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Intelligent-Tiering tracks access for each object and moves it to cheaper tiers when it is not used, then back to the frequent tier when it is, with millisecond access and no retrieval charges.",
      "Popular images would pay a retrieval charge on every request, which can cost more than Standard for objects that are read often.",
      "The company would build the tracking job that it wants to avoid, and one zone storage risks losing data.",
      "Instant Retrieval has the highest retrieval charges of the millisecond classes, which hurts for images that become popular again."] },

{ id: "d4-03", d: 4,
  q: "A research institute publishes a 400 TB climate dataset in Amazon S3 for universities around the world. Download traffic has made data transfer the institute's largest AWS cost. The institute wants the universities, all of which have AWS accounts, to pay for their own downloads while the institute keeps paying for storage.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on Requester Pays for the S3 bucket. Have the universities send authenticated requests that acknowledge the charges.",
      "Turn on Requester Pays for the S3 bucket. Keep allowing anonymous downloads from the universities' websites.",
      "Serve the dataset through Amazon CloudFront. Invoice each university for its share of the CloudFront charges.",
      "Copy the dataset to a bucket in each university's AWS account with S3 Replication to provide local copies."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With Requester Pays, the account that makes the request pays the request and data transfer charges, and the bucket owner keeps paying for storage.",
      "Requester Pays does not allow anonymous requests, because AWS must know which account to charge.",
      "CloudFront still bills the institute, and invoicing each university is manual work outside AWS.",
      "Replication charges the institute for the transfer and multiplies storage across accounts."] },

{ id: "d4-04", d: 4,
  q: "A bank must keep scanned loan documents for 10 years to meet regulations. After the first month, the documents are almost never read. When an auditor does ask for a document, the bank can wait up to 48 hours for it. The bank stores 800 TB today and adds 20 TB each month, and it wants the lowest storage cost.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Store the documents in S3 Glacier Deep Archive after the first month. Use bulk retrievals for audits.",
      "Store the documents in S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after the first month. Use standard retrievals for audits.",
      "Store the documents in S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after the first month to provide fast access for audits.",
      "Store the documents in S3 One Zone-IA after the first month. Copy them to a second Region for durability."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Deep Archive is the lowest-cost S3 storage class for data kept for years, and bulk retrievals finish well within 48 hours at the lowest retrieval price.",
      "Flexible Retrieval costs more to store than Deep Archive, and the bank does not need its faster retrievals.",
      "Instant Retrieval gives millisecond access that the bank does not need, at a much higher storage price.",
      "One Zone-IA costs more than the archive classes, and copying to another Region doubles the storage."] },

{ id: "d4-05", d: 4,
  q: "A hospital keeps medical images in S3 Standard. Each image is read only a few times each year, usually when a patient returns for a visit, but when a doctor opens an image it must appear within milliseconds. The images must be kept for 5 years, and the hospital wants to cut storage costs.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Move the images to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval with an S3 Lifecycle rule to provide millisecond access at a lower price.",
      "Move the images to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval with an S3 Lifecycle rule. Use expedited retrievals when a doctor opens an image.",
      "Move the images to S3 Glacier Deep Archive with an S3 Lifecycle rule. Restore each image when the patient books a visit.",
      "Keep the images in S3 Standard, and compress each image to lower the storage cost."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Instant Retrieval is built for data that is read about once a quarter or less but needs millisecond access, at a much lower storage price than Standard.",
      "Even expedited retrievals take minutes, not milliseconds.",
      "Deep Archive restores take hours, and patients do not always book ahead.",
      "Medical images often compress poorly, and Standard is still the most expensive class for data read a few times each year."] },

{ id: "d4-06", d: 4,
  q: "A company's Amazon EFS file system holds 60 TB of project files. Files are worked on heavily for a few weeks and then rarely opened, but users still need to open old files directly from the same paths without a restore step. Most of the data is more than 90 days old, and all of it is billed at the EFS Standard price.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on EFS lifecycle management to move files that are not accessed for 30 days to Infrequent Access, and back when they are opened.",
      "Copy the files that are older than 90 days to S3 Glacier Deep Archive with AWS DataSync. Delete them from the file system.",
      "Move the file system to EFS One Zone storage to lower the price of all files by keeping every file in a single Availability Zone.",
      "Switch the file system to Provisioned throughput mode to lower the storage price of files that are not accessed."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Lifecycle management moves cold files to the cheaper Infrequent Access class automatically, and the files stay at the same paths. They move back when users open them again.",
      "Users would lose direct access at the same paths and would wait hours for a restore.",
      "One Zone lowers the price but gives up multi-zone durability for all files, and it does not tier cold data.",
      "Throughput mode changes performance and billing for throughput. It does not lower storage prices."] },

{ id: "d4-07", d: 4,
  q: "A company takes monthly Amazon EBS snapshots of its databases and must keep each one for 7 years for compliance. The snapshots are almost never restored, and a restore can take a few days when it is needed. Snapshot storage has become a large part of the AWS bill. The snapshots must stay in the same account and Region.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Move monthly snapshots older than 90 days to the EBS Snapshots Archive tier to provide low-cost long-term storage.",
      "Delete the monthly snapshots older than 90 days and keep only the daily snapshots from the last 90 days.",
      "Copy the monthly snapshots to another AWS Region to provide a cheaper long-term copy. Delete the originals after the copy finishes.",
      "Turn on EBS fast snapshot restore for the monthly snapshots to lower their storage price. Keep the snapshots in the standard tier."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The archive tier stores full snapshots at a much lower price for long-term retention. Restores take hours to days, which the company can accept.",
      "Deleting the monthly snapshots breaks the 7-year compliance requirement.",
      "A copy in another Region adds a second set of storage charges and data transfer.",
      "Fast snapshot restore speeds up restores and adds charges. It does not lower storage prices."] },

{ id: "d4-08", d: 4,
  q: "A company's S3 bill keeps growing, although the amount of data that its applications store has been flat for months. A review shows that a data upload tool often fails partway through large multipart uploads and does not clean up. The company wants to find the waste across all of its buckets and stop paying for it.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Use Amazon S3 Storage Lens to see the bytes in incomplete multipart uploads for each bucket.",
      "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule to each bucket that aborts incomplete multipart uploads a few days after they start.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning for each bucket to keep the partial uploads as previous object versions.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration for each bucket to make uploads finish faster.",
      "Move each bucket to S3 Intelligent-Tiering to lower the price of the uploaded parts. Keep the current upload tool."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Storage Lens reports incomplete multipart upload bytes across accounts and buckets, which shows where the hidden storage is.",
      "The lifecycle action removes the parts of uploads that never finished, so they stop adding charges.",
      "Versioning does not apply to unfinished uploads and can add more storage.",
      "Faster uploads may fail less, but existing parts stay and still cost money.",
      "Parts of unfinished uploads are not objects in a storage class, so this does not remove them."] },

{ id: "d4-09", d: 4,
  q: "A company backs up its on-premises servers to physical tapes with commercial backup software and ships the tapes to an offsite vault. Tape handling is slow and costly. The company wants to keep its backup software and processes but store the backups in AWS at low cost for 10 years.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Deploy AWS Storage Gateway as a Tape Gateway. Point the backup software at the virtual tape library and archive tapes to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.",
      "Deploy Amazon S3 File Gateway. Change the backup software to write backup files to the NFS share, and move the files to S3 Glacier Deep Archive with a lifecycle rule.",
      "Replace the backup software with AWS Backup. Create backup plans for each on-premises server that store backups in a vault.",
      "Copy the backup files to Amazon S3 each night with AWS DataSync. Move them to S3 Glacier Deep Archive with a lifecycle rule."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Tape Gateway presents a virtual tape library that existing backup software already understands, and archived virtual tapes move to low-cost Deep Archive. Processes stay the same.",
      "The backup jobs would change from tape to file targets, which changes the company's processes.",
      "Replacing the backup software changes the processes that the company wants to keep.",
      "This adds a separate copy job and changes how the backups are written and found."] },

{ id: "d4-10", d: 4,
  q: "Thousands of devices each send a 2 KB status record every few seconds. An AWS Lambda function writes each record to Amazon S3 as its own object, which creates more than a billion objects each month. S3 PUT request charges are now higher than storage charges. Analysts query the data with Amazon Athena once a day.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Send the records to Amazon Data Firehose. Buffer them into larger objects before delivery to Amazon S3.",
      "Keep writing one object for each record, but store the objects in S3 Standard-IA. Query the objects with Athena once a day.",
      "Keep writing one object for each record, but turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket. Query the objects with Athena once a day as before.",
      "Send the records to Amazon SQS. Have the Lambda function write each message to Amazon S3 as its own object."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Firehose buffers records by size or time and writes them as far fewer, larger objects, which cuts the number of PUT requests while daily Athena queries still work.",
      "Standard-IA has a higher PUT price and a minimum billable object size, so tiny objects would cost more.",
      "Transfer Acceleration adds a charge and does not reduce the number of requests.",
      "The same number of PUT requests still reaches Amazon S3, so the cost stays the same."] },

{ id: "d4-11", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 50 TB of files on an on-premises NFS server that it must copy to Amazon S3 over its existing internet connection within 3 weeks. The transfer must be scheduled for nights, verify that every file arrives intact, and retry failures without staff watching it. The team has no time to write or test scripts.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Deploy an AWS DataSync agent on premises. Create a task from the NFS share to Amazon S3 with a nightly schedule and data verification turned on.",
      "Write a script that runs the AWS CLI s3 sync command each night from an on-premises server. Check its output and retry failed files each morning.",
      "Mount the NFS share on an Amazon EC2 instance over a VPN. Copy the files to Amazon S3 with rsync from a cron job.",
      "Deploy Amazon S3 File Gateway on premises. Copy the files into its share by hand each night."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DataSync runs scheduled transfers, verifies integrity, retries failures, and reports progress, with no scripts to write or watch.",
      "The team would write and test the script and check every run, which it has no time for.",
      "This adds a VPN, an instance, and custom copy jobs to build and watch.",
      "Copying files by hand each night needs staff and does not verify each file."] },

{ id: "d4-12", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs a development environment that uses an Amazon EFS file system for build caches and test data. The data can be recreated from source control in an hour if it is lost. The environment runs in one Availability Zone, and the company wants to lower its file storage cost.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Move the data to an EFS file system that uses EFS One Zone storage in the same Availability Zone as the environment.",
      "Move the data to an EFS file system that uses EFS Regional storage with Provisioned throughput turned on.",
      "Move the data to a Multi-AZ file system on Amazon FSx for Windows File Server to lower the storage price.",
      "Keep the EFS Regional file system and turn on EFS Replication to a second Region to protect the data."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["One Zone storage costs less than Regional storage by keeping data in one zone. The data can be recreated and the environment uses one zone, so the lower durability is acceptable.",
      "Regional storage with Provisioned throughput costs more, not less.",
      "A Multi-AZ Windows file system costs more and does not suit Linux build hosts.",
      "Replication adds a second copy and more cost for data that can be recreated."] },

{ id: "d4-13", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs a steady 24/7 baseline of Amazon EC2 instances for its web tier, and it expects to change instance families and move some workloads to AWS Fargate and AWS Lambda over the next 3 years. It also runs nightly batch jobs that can be stopped and restarted at any time. All workloads run On-Demand today.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Purchase a Compute Savings Plan for the steady baseline usage.",
      "Run the nightly batch jobs on EC2 Spot Instances.",
      "Purchase EC2 Instance Savings Plans for the current instance family for the baseline.",
      "Purchase Standard Reserved Instances for the current instance types for the batch jobs.",
      "Run the steady web tier on EC2 Spot Instances to get the largest discount. Keep the batch jobs On-Demand."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Compute Savings Plans apply to EC2 usage of any family and Region, and to Fargate and Lambda, so the discount follows the planned changes.",
      "Interruptible batch jobs are a good fit for Spot Instances, which cost far less than On-Demand.",
      "EC2 Instance Savings Plans are tied to one instance family in one Region, so they stop applying when the company changes families or moves to Fargate.",
      "The batch jobs run only at night, so reservations would sit unused most of the day.",
      "Spot Instances can be interrupted, which is not suitable for a web tier that must run all the time."] },

{ id: "d4-14", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs 30 m6i.xlarge Amazon EC2 instances in eu-west-1 around the clock for a stable core application. The company is confident that it will keep this instance family and Region for at least 3 years and wants the largest discount that still allows changes to instance size and operating system.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Purchase an EC2 Instance Savings Plan for the m6i family in eu-west-1 with a 3-year term. Keep the instances in eu-west-1.",
      "Purchase a Compute Savings Plan with a 3-year term for the same hourly commitment. Apply it to the m6i instances in eu-west-1.",
      "Run the application on Spot Instances with a capacity-optimized allocation strategy.",
      "Purchase On-Demand Capacity Reservations for the 30 instances for 3 years. Keep paying On-Demand rates for the usage."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["EC2 Instance Savings Plans give the largest Savings Plans discount for one instance family in one Region, and they still apply across sizes and operating systems within that family.",
      "Compute Savings Plans are more flexible but give a smaller discount, and the company does not need that flexibility.",
      "Spot Instances can be interrupted, which does not suit a stable core application.",
      "Capacity Reservations reserve capacity at On-Demand prices and give no discount by themselves."] },

{ id: "d4-15", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs a large nightly rendering job on 200 Amazon EC2 instances. Each frame is rendered independently, progress is saved to Amazon S3 after each frame, and the job must finish by morning but can take longer during the night. The company wants to reduce compute costs as much as possible.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Run the job on Spot Instances across many instance types and Availability Zones with price-capacity-optimized allocation.",
      "Run the job on On-Demand Instances that a 1-year EC2 Instance Savings Plan covers to lower the hourly price.",
      "Run the job on Spot Instances of a single instance type in a single Availability Zone to keep the fleet configuration simple.",
      "Run the job on Dedicated Instances, which are not interrupted by the needs of other AWS customers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Interrupted frames can be rendered again, so Spot Instances fit well. Spreading across many instance types and zones with price-capacity-optimized allocation lowers both cost and the chance of interruptions.",
      "A Savings Plan for a job that runs only at night pays for hours that are not used.",
      "One instance type in one zone has a smaller capacity pool, which raises the chance of interruptions and delays.",
      "Dedicated Instances cost more than On-Demand, and the job can tolerate interruptions."] },

{ id: "d4-16", d: 4,
  q: "A stateless web application runs in an Auto Scaling group of On-Demand Instances. At least 4 instances are always needed to serve base traffic, and peaks need up to 20. The application handles losing an instance without errors. The company wants to cut compute costs while keeping the base capacity reliable.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Use a mixed instances policy with an On-Demand base capacity of 4 and Spot Instances for capacity above the base, across several instance types.",
      "Use a mixed instances policy with Spot Instances for all capacity, including the base, across several instance types.",
      "Keep all capacity On-Demand, and lower the maximum size of the group from 20 instances to 10 to limit spending.",
      "Use a mixed instances policy with an On-Demand base capacity of 4 and Spot Instances of a single instance type for all of the capacity above that base."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The On-Demand base keeps the 4 needed instances reliable, and Spot covers peak capacity at a much lower price. Several instance types give Spot more capacity pools to draw from.",
      "If Spot capacity is reclaimed, the base could drop below the 4 instances that the application always needs.",
      "A lower maximum saves money by failing to serve peak traffic.",
      "A single instance type makes Spot interruptions and shortages more likely during peaks."] },

{ id: "d4-17", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs hundreds of Amazon EC2 instances, Auto Scaling groups, and Amazon EBS volumes that teams sized years ago. The company suspects that many resources are larger than they need to be, but it has no time to analyze utilization metrics for each one. It wants specific recommendations for smaller or cheaper resources.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on AWS Compute Optimizer for the accounts and review its rightsizing recommendations for the instances, groups, and volumes.",
      "Export the CloudWatch metrics of every resource to a spreadsheet each month. Compare the utilization of each one by hand to find oversized resources.",
      "Use AWS Cost Explorer to find the accounts with the highest EC2 spend and cut their instance sizes in half.",
      "Turn on AWS Config for all resources and review the configuration history of each instance and volume."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Compute Optimizer analyzes utilization metrics with machine learning and recommends specific instance types, group settings, and volume types, with no manual analysis.",
      "This is the manual analysis that the company has no time for.",
      "Spending by account does not show which resources are oversized, and halving sizes at random can break workloads.",
      "Config records configuration changes, not utilization, so it does not show which resources are oversized."] },

{ id: "d4-18", d: 4,
  q: "Developers use large Amazon EC2 instances as workstations during business hours. Each workstation takes 20 minutes after boot to load a large in-memory dataset before it can be used. The company wants to stop paying for instance hours at night but does not want developers to wait 20 minutes every morning.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on hibernation for the instances. Hibernate them each evening and start them each morning.",
      "Stop the instances each evening and start them each morning. Accept the reload time.",
      "Terminate the instances each evening and launch new ones from the AMI each morning.",
      "Keep the instances running at night with a 1-year EC2 Instance Savings Plan to lower the hourly price."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Hibernation saves the contents of memory to the EBS root volume. Instance hours stop at night, and the dataset is back in memory as soon as the instance resumes.",
      "A normal stop clears memory, so developers would wait 20 minutes each morning.",
      "New instances start empty, so the dataset would load again, and local changes would be lost.",
      "A Savings Plan lowers the price, but the company still pays for every hour at night."] },

{ id: "d4-19", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs an internal API on two Amazon EC2 instances 24/7. The API receives about 5,000 requests each day, mostly during business hours, and each request takes less than 1 second to process. The instances sit idle most of the time, and the company wants to lower the cost of the API without changing its interface.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Move the API to Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda, which charge only for the requests that arrive.",
      "Keep the API on the two EC2 instances. Buy a 3-year EC2 Instance Savings Plan that covers both of them.",
      "Move the API to AWS Lambda behind Amazon API Gateway, with provisioned concurrency that keeps two environments warm all day.",
      "Move the API to two smaller EC2 instances and keep them running all the time."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With Lambda and API Gateway, the company pays per request and per duration, which costs very little for a few thousand short requests each day, and the API keeps the same interface.",
      "A Savings Plan lowers the hourly price, but the company still pays for idle instances around the clock.",
      "Provisioned concurrency charges for the warm environments every hour, including the many idle hours, which works against the goal.",
      "Smaller instances cost less but still sit idle most of the time."] },

{ id: "d4-20", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs a fleet of Java microservices on Amazon EC2 m5 instances. The services run on Linux and use no x86-specific libraries. The company wants better price performance for the fleet with as little change to the services as possible. The fleet runs around the clock on about 120 instances and is the company's largest compute cost.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Move the services to AWS Graviton-based EC2 instances, such as the m7g family, after testing them on the Arm architecture.",
      "Move the services to larger EC2 m5 instances to finish each request faster and run fewer instances.",
      "Move the services to x86 compute optimized EC2 c5 instances to provide more CPU for each dollar for every service.",
      "Move the services to burstable EC2 t3 instances in standard mode to lower the hourly price."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Graviton instances use Arm-based processors with better price performance than comparable x86 instances, and Java on Linux usually runs with little or no change.",
      "Larger instances cost more per hour, and fewer of them may not offset the price.",
      "Compute optimized instances have less memory for each vCPU, which may not suit every service, and they do not improve price performance as much.",
      "Burstable instances run short of CPU credits under steady load, which hurts performance."] },

{ id: "d4-21", d: 4,
  q: "A company hosts 12 small internal web applications. Each application has its own Application Load Balancer, its own DNS name under example.com, and very little traffic. Load balancer hourly charges are now larger than the compute cost of the applications. All applications use HTTPS and run in the same VPC. Each application has its own host name and TLS certificate.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Replace the 12 load balancers with one Application Load Balancer. Add host-based listener rules that forward each host name to the application's target group.",
      "Replace the 12 load balancers with one Network Load Balancer. Add a TLS listener on a different port for each application to keep the traffic of each application apart.",
      "Replace the 12 load balancers with Amazon Route 53 weighted records that point directly at the instances of each application.",
      "Keep the 12 load balancers and purchase a Savings Plan to lower their hourly charges."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["One Application Load Balancer can route requests by host name to many target groups, so the company pays one set of hourly charges instead of 12.",
      "Users would need a different port for each application, and one TLS certificate setup per port adds work that host rules avoid.",
      "DNS records do not check the health of instances in the same way or end TLS, and each instance would need its own certificate.",
      "Savings Plans do not apply to Elastic Load Balancing charges."] },

{ id: "d4-22", d: 4,
  q: "A company copied its production architecture to build a development environment: a VPC across three Availability Zones with a NAT gateway in each zone, and a PostgreSQL database on Amazon RDS with Multi-AZ turned on. The development environment can be unavailable for a few hours if a zone fails, and the company wants to lower its cost.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Use one NAT gateway in one Availability Zone for all private subnets in the development VPC.",
      "Change the development DB instance to a Single-AZ deployment.",
      "Replace the NAT gateways with an internet gateway route in each private subnet to provide free outbound access.",
      "Add a read replica to the development DB instance to take load off the primary. Keep the Multi-AZ deployment.",
      "Purchase Reserved Instances for the three NAT gateways in the development VPC. Keep one NAT gateway in each zone."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["One shared NAT gateway removes two sets of hourly charges. Losing outbound access during a zone failure is acceptable for development.",
      "A Single-AZ DB instance costs about half as much as Multi-AZ, and the environment can tolerate the downtime.",
      "Private subnets with an internet gateway route would need public IP addresses, which changes the network design and exposes the instances.",
      "A read replica adds another DB instance and more cost.",
      "NAT gateways cannot be reserved, so this does not lower their charges."] },

{ id: "d4-23", d: 4,
  q: "A company has one AWS account shared by five product teams. Finance wants each team to see its own monthly spending and to get an alert when a team's spending is forecast to exceed its budget. All of the teams' resources are already tagged with a Team tag. Finance does not want to split the account into several accounts.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Activate the Team tag as a user-defined cost allocation tag for the account.",
      "Create an AWS Budgets cost budget for each team, filtered by the Team tag, with an alert on forecasted spend.",
      "Create an Amazon CloudWatch billing alarm for each team, filtered by the Team tag on the EstimatedCharges metric.",
      "Create one AWS Budgets cost budget for the whole account with an alert on forecasted spend.",
      "Move each team into its own AWS account to provide a separate bill for each team."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["User-defined tags must be activated as cost allocation tags before billing tools can group and filter costs by them.",
      "Budgets can filter costs by a cost allocation tag and alert when forecasted spending passes the budget.",
      "The EstimatedCharges metric is not split by resource tag, so it cannot track each team.",
      "One budget for the whole account does not show each team's spending or alert on each team's budget.",
      "Separate accounts would work but are a large change that the tags already make unnecessary."] },

{ id: "d4-24", d: 4,
  q: "A finance team needs hourly, resource-level cost and usage data for all accounts in the company's organization. The team wants to run its own SQL queries on the data, join it with internal cost center tables, and keep the history for years. The team does not want to build its own data collection.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Cost and Usage Report (CUR) export to Amazon S3 in Parquet format. Query it with Amazon Athena.",
      "Download the monthly bill as a PDF from the Billing console each month. Load the totals into a spreadsheet.",
      "Use AWS Cost Explorer reports for each account. Copy the charts into the finance team's reports each week.",
      "Write an AWS Lambda function that calls the Cost Explorer API every hour. Store the results in Amazon S3 for Amazon Athena to query."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The Cost and Usage Report delivers detailed, hourly, resource-level data to S3 automatically, and Athena can query it with SQL and join it with other tables.",
      "A PDF bill has no hourly or resource-level detail and needs manual work every month.",
      "Cost Explorer is good for visual analysis, but copying charts by hand does not give SQL access to detailed data.",
      "This builds the custom data collection that the team wants to avoid, and each Cost Explorer API request has a charge."] },

{ id: "d4-25", d: 4,
  q: "A company runs 15 development and test databases on provisioned Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL DB instances. Each database is used for a few hours on some weekdays and sits idle the rest of the time, including every night and weekend. The developers accept a short delay when a database is used again after a long idle period.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Change the databases to Aurora Serverless with a minimum capacity of 0 ACUs and automatic pause turned on.",
      "Change the databases to Aurora Serverless with a minimum capacity of 8 ACUs to keep them ready for developers.",
      "Keep the provisioned DB instances and purchase Reserved Instances for all 15 databases to lower the hourly price.",
      "Keep the provisioned DB instances and move them to the largest instance class to finish the tests sooner."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With a minimum of 0 ACUs, Aurora Serverless pauses idle databases and charges only for storage while paused, which suits databases that are idle most of the time.",
      "A high minimum keeps paying for capacity during the long idle periods.",
      "Reserved Instances lower the price of instances that run all the time, but these databases are idle most of the time.",
      "Larger instances cost more per hour and still run while idle."] },

{ id: "d4-26", d: 4,
  q: "A company's Amazon DynamoDB table uses provisioned capacity mode with a steady load of about 20,000 read capacity units and 5,000 write capacity units, every hour of every day. The load has been stable for two years and is expected to stay the same for at least another year. Finance has approved a commitment of up to three years.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Purchase DynamoDB reserved capacity for the steady read and write capacity. Keep the table in provisioned capacity mode.",
      "Switch the table to on-demand capacity mode to pay only for the requests that are made. Keep the current table class.",
      "Turn on DynamoDB auto scaling with a target of 90% to trim the provisioned capacity. Set the minimum to the steady load.",
      "Switch the table to the DynamoDB Standard-IA table class to lower the price of reads and writes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Reserved capacity gives a large discount on provisioned capacity in exchange for a one-year or three-year commitment, which fits a steady, predictable load.",
      "On-demand pricing costs more than provisioned capacity for steady, predictable traffic.",
      "Auto scaling helps when load changes, but this load is flat, so it saves little compared with a reservation.",
      "Standard-IA lowers storage prices but raises the price of reads and writes."] },

{ id: "d4-27", d: 4,
  q: "An application stores user session records in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Each record is needed for 24 hours and then is never read again, but the records are never deleted, so the table has grown to 4 TB. The company wants old records removed automatically without using write capacity for the deletes.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Add an expiration timestamp attribute to each record. Turn on DynamoDB Time to Live (TTL) on that attribute.",
      "Run an AWS Lambda function every hour that scans the DynamoDB table and deletes the records that are older than 24 hours.",
      "Create a new DynamoDB table each day. Delete the table from two days earlier with a scheduled script.",
      "Export the table to Amazon S3 each day. Delete the exported records with BatchWriteItem calls."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["TTL deletes expired items in the background at no charge for writes, with no code to maintain.",
      "Scans and deletes use read and write capacity, and the function must be written and maintained.",
      "Rotating tables needs changes to the application and a script to maintain.",
      "This adds exports and custom deletes, which use write capacity."] },

{ id: "d4-28", d: 4,
  q: "A company's production MySQL database runs on Amazon RDS as a Multi-AZ deployment. It has used the same db.r6g.2xlarge instance class 24/7 for two years. The company expects the database to keep the same size and engine for at least three more years, and it wants to lower the database's compute costs.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Purchase a 3-year Reserved Instance for the DB instance class and Multi-AZ deployment that the database uses.",
      "Purchase a 3-year Compute Savings Plan to provide a discount on the DB instance. Apply the plan to all compute usage.",
      "Purchase a 3-year Reserved Instance for a Single-AZ deployment of the same DB instance class.",
      "Move the database to Amazon EC2 Spot Instances and run MySQL on them to provide a much lower hourly price for the database."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["RDS Reserved Instances give a large discount for a one-year or three-year commitment to a DB instance class, which fits a steady production database.",
      "Compute Savings Plans apply to EC2, Fargate, and Lambda, not to Amazon RDS.",
      "A Single-AZ reservation covers only half of the usage of a Multi-AZ DB instance, so the discount would not apply in full.",
      "Spot Instances can be interrupted, and the company would manage MySQL itself."] },

{ id: "d4-29", d: 4,
  q: "A company provisions large amounts of storage for its Amazon RDS databases up front because running out of space once caused an outage. Most databases use less than 30% of their allocated storage, but growth is hard to predict. The company wants to allocate less storage while still avoiding outages from full disks.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Allocate storage closer to current needs. Turn on RDS storage autoscaling with a maximum storage threshold.",
      "Allocate storage closer to current needs. Create a CloudWatch alarm that emails the team to add RDS storage by hand.",
      "Keep the current allocation. Move the RDS databases to a cheaper storage type such as magnetic storage.",
      "Allocate storage closer to current needs. Run a weekly script that checks free space and resizes each RDS database."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Storage autoscaling grows the storage automatically when free space runs low, up to the set maximum, so databases can start smaller without risking full disks.",
      "This depends on someone acting on each alarm in time, which is the risk that caused the outage.",
      "Magnetic storage is a previous-generation option with poor performance, and the databases stay oversized.",
      "A weekly script can miss fast growth and must be built and maintained."] },

{ id: "d4-30", d: 4,
  q: "A company keeps 18 months of order history in an Amazon DynamoDB table. Orders older than 30 days are read only a few times each month for customer service, but they make up 90% of the table's data. Storage is now the largest part of the table's cost, and the application must keep querying the same table.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Change the table to the DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access (Standard-IA) table class.",
      "Change the table to on-demand capacity mode to lower the cost of reads and writes.",
      "Export orders older than 30 days to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive and delete them from the table.",
      "Cache reads from the table in a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster to lower the cost of reads."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The Standard-IA table class lowers storage prices for tables whose storage cost is high compared with their throughput, and the application keeps using the same table.",
      "Capacity mode changes how throughput is billed, not the price of storage.",
      "Customer service could no longer query old orders in the table, and restores from Deep Archive take hours.",
      "DAX adds the cost of a cache cluster and does not lower storage costs."] },

{ id: "d4-31", d: 4,
  q: "Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets process large files that they read from and write to Amazon S3 in the same Region, about 80 TB each month. All of this traffic goes through NAT gateways, and NAT gateway data processing charges have become one of the company's largest network costs. The instances must stay in private subnets.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Create an S3 gateway endpoint and add it to the route tables of the private subnets.",
      "Create an S3 interface endpoint in each private subnet and turn on private DNS for it.",
      "Replace the NAT gateways with NAT instances on smaller EC2 instance types to provide the same outbound access.",
      "Move the instances to public subnets and give each one a public IP address to provide direct internet access."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Gateway endpoints for S3 have no hourly or data processing charges, and traffic to S3 in the same Region stops passing through the NAT gateways.",
      "Interface endpoints remove the NAT charges but add hourly and per-GB endpoint charges, which cost more than a gateway endpoint.",
      "NAT instances are a single point of failure to manage, and the S3 traffic would still pass through them.",
      "This exposes the instances to the internet and is a large change to the network design."] },

{ id: "d4-32", d: 4,
  q: "A company's analytics cluster runs on Amazon EC2 in us-west-2 and reads 200 TB each month from an Amazon S3 bucket in eu-central-1, where the data must stay for regulatory reasons. Cross-Region data transfer charges are now higher than the cost of the cluster itself. The analysis results are small, and a team in us-west-2 reads them.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Run the analytics cluster in eu-central-1, next to the S3 bucket. Send only the small results to us-west-2.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket to provide faster, cheaper transfers to us-west-2.",
      "Copy the bucket to us-west-2 with S3 Cross-Region Replication and run the cluster there. Delete the copy each month after the analysis.",
      "Serve the bucket through an Amazon CloudFront distribution to provide a cache for the cluster's reads."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Processing the data in the Region where it is stored removes the cross-Region transfer of 200 TB each month, and the data stays in eu-central-1.",
      "Transfer Acceleration adds a charge on top of data transfer and does not remove the cross-Region transfer.",
      "Replication still transfers the data across Regions and puts a copy outside eu-central-1, which regulations do not allow.",
      "The cluster reads different data each time, so a cache saves little, and CloudFront adds its own charges."] },

{ id: "d4-33", d: 4,
  q: "A software company lets customers download installer files of 1 to 4 GB from an Amazon S3 bucket in us-east-1. Customers around the world download about 300 TB each month, and the same few installers make up most downloads. Data transfer out from Amazon S3 is the company's largest AWS cost.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Serve the downloads through an Amazon CloudFront distribution with the bucket as its origin. Use origin access control for the bucket.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket and give customers the accelerate endpoint.",
      "Copy the installers to S3 buckets in five Regions with S3 Cross-Region Replication. Route each customer to the nearest bucket with Route 53.",
      "Move the installers to S3 Standard-IA to lower the price of each download. Keep serving the downloads from the bucket."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Data transfer from S3 to CloudFront has no charge, and CloudFront data transfer out costs less than S3 data transfer out. Caching popular installers at edge locations also speeds up downloads.",
      "Transfer Acceleration adds a charge to each transfer and does not cache the files.",
      "Replication adds storage and replication charges in five Regions while each download still pays S3 data transfer out.",
      "Standard-IA adds a retrieval charge to each download and does not lower data transfer prices."] },

{ id: "d4-34", d: 4,
  q: "A company has two VPCs in the same Region that exchange about 500 TB of data each month between instances in the same Availability Zones. There are no other VPCs, and none are planned. The VPCs are connected through an AWS Transit Gateway today, and its data processing charges are high.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Connect the two VPCs with VPC peering instead of the transit gateway. Update the route tables in both VPCs.",
      "Connect the two VPCs over AWS Site-to-Site VPN instead of the transit gateway.",
      "Keep the transit gateway and send the traffic through NAT gateways in each VPC. Add a route to each NAT gateway.",
      "Keep the transit gateway and add a second transit gateway to split the traffic. Split the VPC attachments between them."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["VPC peering has no hourly charge and no data processing charge, and traffic between instances in the same Availability Zone over a peering connection is free. With only two VPCs, the hub of a transit gateway adds cost but no value.",
      "A VPN between two VPCs adds hourly charges and limits bandwidth for 500 TB each month.",
      "NAT gateways add their own data processing charges and do not route traffic between VPCs.",
      "A second transit gateway doubles the hourly charges and still charges for data processing."] },

{ id: "d4-35", d: 4,
  q: "An order processing service receives about 100 orders each second on average, but marketing emails cause short bursts of up to 2,000 orders each second. The backend instances are sized for the bursts and sit mostly idle. Orders can be processed up to 15 minutes after they arrive, and the company wants to lower the backend cost.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Send orders to an Amazon SQS queue, and size the backend for the average order rate.",
      "Keep the backend sized for the bursts. Purchase a Savings Plan that lowers the hourly price of the instances.",
      "Send orders to an Amazon SQS queue. Keep the backend sized for the largest burst.",
      "Turn on API Gateway throttling at 100 requests each second and reject the extra orders."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The queue holds orders during bursts, so a smaller backend works through the backlog within the allowed 15 minutes. The company stops paying for idle burst capacity.",
      "A discount still pays for capacity that sits idle most of the time.",
      "The queue protects the orders, but a backend sized for the largest burst still sits idle most of the time and costs the same.",
      "Rejecting orders loses business, which the company does not want."] },

{ id: "d4-36", d: 4,
  q: "A company connects its data center to AWS through one AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection to a virtual private gateway. Traffic has grown to about 3 Gbps, and the VPN tunnels cannot carry it. The company is not ready to order AWS Direct Connect and wants more bandwidth at low cost.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Attach the VPC to an AWS Transit Gateway, and terminate several Site-to-Site VPN connections on the transit gateway.",
      "Turn on equal-cost multi-path (ECMP) routing on the transit gateway, and advertise the same routes over all of the VPN tunnels.",
      "Add more VPN connections to the virtual private gateway. Spread traffic across them with static routes.",
      "Change the VPN tunnels to use larger maximum transmission units (MTUs) to provide more bandwidth. Keep one VPN connection on the virtual private gateway.",
      "Replace the VPN with AWS Client VPN to provide more bandwidth for each user. Give each server in the data center a client profile."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["A transit gateway can terminate several VPN connections, which provides more tunnels for the traffic.",
      "ECMP spreads traffic across the tunnels, so the bandwidth adds up beyond the limit of a single tunnel.",
      "A virtual private gateway does not use ECMP across VPN connections, so the bandwidth does not add up.",
      "The MTU is not a bandwidth setting, and each tunnel keeps its throughput limit.",
      "Client VPN connects individual users, not a data center network."] },

{ id: "d4-37", d: 4,
  q: "A company has 12 AWS accounts that each pay their own bills with separate credit cards. Finance wants one monthly bill for all accounts and wants the combined usage of all accounts to count toward volume pricing tiers, such as the tiers for Amazon S3 storage. The accounts must stay separate.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?",
  o: ["Create an organization in AWS Organizations, invite the 12 accounts. Use consolidated billing. Keep each account's own resources in place.",
      "Move all workloads into one AWS account to combine the usage for volume pricing. Close the other 11 accounts.",
      "Export each account's bill each month and combine them in a spreadsheet for finance. Pay each bill with its own card.",
      "Create a cost allocation tag in each account with the same name to combine the bills. Use the tag to group costs in Cost Explorer."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Consolidated billing gives one bill for all member accounts and combines their usage for volume pricing tiers, while each account stays separate.",
      "The accounts must stay separate, and merging them is a large migration.",
      "A spreadsheet combines the numbers by hand but gives neither one bill nor combined volume pricing.",
      "Tags group costs inside an account. They do not combine bills or usage across accounts."] },

{ id: "d4-38", d: 4,
  q: "A company's internal timesheet application runs on Amazon EC2 instances that an Auto Scaling group manages. Employees use it only on weekdays from 8 AM to 6 PM local time, and nobody uses it at night or on weekends. The group runs 6 instances at all times, and the company wants to stop paying for unused instance hours.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add scheduled actions that scale the group to 0 each weekday evening and back to 6 each weekday morning.",
      "Add an Auto Scaling target tracking policy on average CPU utilization. Keep the minimum capacity of the group at 6 instances.",
      "Purchase EC2 Instance Savings Plans for the 6 instances to lower the hourly price that the company pays.",
      "Replace the instances with Spot Instances. Keep 6 instances running at all times to serve employees."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Scheduled actions change capacity at known times, so the company pays for instances only during business hours.",
      "A minimum of 6 keeps all instances running, so nothing changes at night.",
      "A Savings Plan lowers the price but still pays for every hour at night and on weekends.",
      "Spot lowers the price but still pays for idle hours, and interruptions can affect employees."] },

{ id: "d4-39", d: 4,
  q: "A company sends about 50 TB of processed data each month from AWS to its on-premises data warehouse over the internet. Transfers often slow down at busy times, and data transfer out charges are high. The company expects this volume to keep growing for years. The data warehouse team needs predictable transfer times to plan its nightly loads.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Set up an AWS Direct Connect connection to the data center. Send the data over a private virtual interface. Size the connection for the expected growth.",
      "Set up a second AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection to double the bandwidth of the internet transfers. Keep the existing internet connection.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket that holds the processed data. Send the data through the accelerate endpoint.",
      "Compress the data with a stronger algorithm and keep sending it over the internet. Schedule the transfers outside busy hours."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Direct Connect gives consistent bandwidth and has lower data transfer out rates than the internet, which pays off for large, steady, growing volumes.",
      "VPN traffic still crosses the internet with the same congestion and internet data transfer out rates.",
      "Transfer Acceleration speeds up long-distance transfers to and from S3 but adds charges and still uses the internet.",
      "Compression helps a little, but the transfers still cross a congested internet path at internet rates."] },

{ id: "d4-40", d: 4,
  q: "A company's marketing website consists only of HTML pages, images, and JavaScript files that change a few times each week. It is served by six Amazon EC2 instances in two Availability Zones with an Application Load Balancer, and visitors come from all over the world. Hosting costs must drop while the site stays fast and highly available.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
  o: ["Host the site in an Amazon S3 bucket and serve it through an Amazon CloudFront distribution.",
      "Move the site to two smaller EC2 instances behind the Application Load Balancer to provide the same pages.",
      "Host the site on the EC2 instances and purchase a 3-year Savings Plan for them.",
      "Host the site in an Amazon S3 bucket in each of three Regions, with Amazon Route 53 latency records."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Static files need no servers. S3 stores them durably, and CloudFront caches them close to visitors, which costs far less than six instances and a load balancer.",
      "Smaller instances still cost more than S3 and CloudFront for static files, and visitors far away still see high latency.",
      "A discount still pays for servers and a load balancer that static files do not need.",
      "Three buckets triple the storage and the publishing work without the caching of a CDN."] },
]);
